---

<GuideLayout
  title="Make Blackboard feel at home in Safari"
  description="Install Glaze on your Mac, allow it on your school’s Blackboard Ultra site, and set up your palette, fonts, background and helpers."
  path="/guides/glaze-safari"
  project="Glaze"
  duration="8 min read"
  updated="2026-10-14"
  chapters={chapters}
>
  <section class="guide-section" id="prepare">
    <h2>Check your Mac and Blackboard</h2>
    <p>Sign in to Blackboard as usual and open Courses. The address should contain <code>/ultra/</code>: Glaze works on any school’s Ultra pages (<code>https://*/ultra/*</code>), with nothing to configure. Login pages and Classic Blackboard keep their own look.</p>
    <div class="guide-table-wrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Requirement</th><th scope="col">What to check</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Mac</th><td>Intel or Apple Silicon, macOS 12 or newer.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Safari</th><td>17.2 or newer (Safari → About Safari). The frosted-glass blur needs Safari 18.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Blackboard</th><td>Your school’s HTTPS Blackboard Ultra site.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>Using Chrome? Follow the <a href="/guides/glaze-chrome">Chrome guide</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-section" id="install">
    <h2>Install the Safari app</h2>
    <p><a href={release}>Glaze 2.1.4</a> is a signed, Apple-notarized Mac app that installs itself.</p>
    <ol class="guide-steps">
      <li><a href={dmg}>Download glaze-2.1.4-safari-macos.dmg</a> and open it.</li>
      <li>Open <strong>Glaze</strong> and click <strong>Install Glaze</strong>. It copies itself into Applications (your own Applications folder if you aren’t an administrator) and reopens from there.</li>
      <li>Glaze opens Safari’s Extensions settings beside a two-step guide, covered next.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Leave Glaze.app in Applications: Safari loads the extension from it, but it doesn’t need to be running. The release includes a <code>.sha256</code> file for checking the download. If macOS says the app is damaged, download it again; don’t bypass the check in Terminal.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-section" id="permissions">
    <h2>Enable Glaze for your school</h2>
    <ol class="guide-steps">
      <li>In <strong>Safari → Settings → Extensions</strong>, tick <strong>Glaze for Blackboard Ultra</strong>. The app’s guide ticks off this step by itself.</li>
      <li>In your Blackboard tab, click the Glaze toolbar button and choose <strong>Always Allow on This Website</strong>.</li>
      <li>Reload the tab.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>To review access later, select Glaze in Extensions settings and choose <strong>Edit Websites</strong>. Safari’s prompt covers the whole site, but Glaze only restyles its <code>/ultra/</code> pages. With Safari profiles, enable Glaze in the profile you use for school; for private windows, turn on <strong>Allow in Private Browsing</strong>. Apple explains these controls in its <a href={apple}>Safari extensions guide</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-section" id="first-theme">
    <h2>Try your first palette</h2>
    <p>Click Glaze in the toolbar. The popup has the theme switch, eight <strong>Looks</strong>, six palettes (<strong>Frost, Midnight, Forest, Ember, Paper, Rose</strong>), three motion levels and <strong>Open the editor</strong>. Changes apply to open Blackboard tabs at once.</p>
    <p>A look sets a palette, fonts, shape and background chosen to go together: Aurora Night, Library, Terminal, Lava Lounge, Synthwave, Clear, Lagoon or Rose Garden. Start from one and adjust it in the editor.</p>
    <figure class="guide-figure guide-figure-compact">
      <a href="/projects/glaze-popup.webp" aria-label="Open the full-size Glaze popup capture"><img src="/projects/glaze-popup.webp" alt="Glaze’s popup with palette swatches, motion levels and Open the editor" width="320" height="297" loading="lazy" /></a>
      <figcaption>The Glaze popup.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>Switch Glaze off and on to compare with Blackboard’s own look; your settings stay. Picking a swatch turns off automatic light/dark switching.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-section" id="editor">
    <h2>Fonts and reading</h2>
    <p>The editor puts every setting beside a live preview of a sample course and saves as you go. Type in <strong>Search settings</strong> at the top (or press <kbd>/</kbd>) to find one.</p>
    <figure class="guide-figure">
      <a href="/projects/glaze-editor.webp" aria-label="Open the full-size Glaze editor capture"><img src="/projects/glaze-editor.webp" alt="Glaze editor with palette and look controls beside its sample course preview" width="1440" height="1000" loading="lazy" /></a>
      <figcaption>The editor and its sample course.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>Under <strong>Look → Fonts</strong>, pick from about forty, each shown in its own face: reading faces such as Lexend, OpenDyslexic and Atkinson Hyperlegible Next, sans, serif and monospace families, and four Nerd Fonts. You can also type any Google Font’s name, or a font installed on your Mac. Headings and code can have their own font. Web fonts download only once you choose one.</p>
    <p>Raise <strong>Text size</strong> and <strong>Line height</strong> if reading feels cramped, and try <strong>Roomy</strong> density. <strong>Corner radius</strong>, <strong>Depth</strong> and <strong>Glass blur</strong> shape the panels.</p>
    <p>Under <strong>Palette</strong>, change any of the fourteen colors and watch the contrast checks. Name it and choose <strong>Save as preset</strong> to keep it (up to twelve).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-section" id="automatic">
    <h2>Light, dark, background and motion</h2>
    <p>In <strong>Palette</strong>, turn on <strong>Follow the system’s light and dark mode</strong> and pick a palette for each (Paper and Frost to start).</p>
    <p>Under <strong>Background</strong>, choose:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Animated:</strong> eight slow scenes (Mist, Orbs, Borealis, Lava, Waves, Dust, Contours, Horizon), with one <strong>Intensity</strong> slider. They pause in background tabs and hold still when motion is off.</li>
      <li><strong>Gradient:</strong> linear, radial, conic or mesh, with angle, blend and spread.</li>
      <li><strong>Aurora</strong>, <strong>Solid</strong>, or <strong>Image</strong> from an <code>https://</code> address, with Dim and Blur.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Backgrounds use your palette’s colors, a ready-made set, or three of your own.</p>
    <p><strong>Motion</strong> is Off, Subtle (default) or Expressive. With <strong>Turn motion off when the system asks for reduced motion</strong> on, your Mac’s setting wins.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-section" id="courses">
    <h2>Courses and extras</h2>
    <p>Glaze softens Ultra’s course colors to fit your palette. Every course you haven’t hidden appears in the editor’s <strong>Courses</strong> section once you’ve opened Blackboard, organizations listed apart. Each has its own color, even where Blackboard repeats one, and you can change it. Turn on <strong>Tint the theme inside a course</strong> and tick courses to give each its own accent while you’re in it.</p>
    <p>Under <strong>Extras</strong>, these start on:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Due-date status</strong>: “in 2 days · Unopened”, “Submitted”, “Submitted late”, “Graded · 18/20”.</li>
      <li><strong>Grades on course cards</strong>, where your instructor shares the overall grade, and <strong>score bars</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>New-grade</strong> and <strong>new-content badges</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>What-if calculator</strong> on a course’s gradebook: the score you’d need on what’s left.</li>
      <li><strong>Command palette</strong>: <kbd>⌘K</kbd> jumps to a course, item, page or setting.</li>
      <li><strong>Download all</strong>: a folder’s files as one zip. Items you haven’t opened are left out, so nothing gets marked as viewed.</li>
      <li><strong>Back up what you type</strong>: if a page reloads or times out before you post, Glaze offers your text back.</li>
      <li><strong>Folder previews</strong>, <strong>course progress</strong>, and <strong>focus mode</strong> for course documents.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Off until you want them: due-date urgency, shorter descriptions, reading width, keyboard shortcuts, dark documents (dark PDFs on dark palettes), and <strong>Declutter</strong> for page parts you don’t use.</p>
    <p>Glaze writes in the language Blackboard is shown in: English, Spanish, French, German, Portuguese (Brazil) or Dutch.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-section" id="backup">
    <h2>Share and back up</h2>
    <p>In <strong>Share</strong>, <strong>Copy theme code</strong> gives your palette, fonts, look and background as one line of text. Paste someone’s code, check it with <strong>Preview</strong>, then choose <strong>Use this theme</strong> or <strong>Cancel</strong>.</p>
    <p><strong>Export settings</strong> saves everything to <code>glaze-settings.json</code>; <strong>Import settings…</strong> replaces your setup with a file’s. Use them to move between Macs or to Chrome. <strong>Reset everything</strong> needs a second click and keeps your saved palettes.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-section" id="privacy">
    <h2>Know what stays on your Mac</h2>
    <p>Glaze has no server and no analytics. Settings, which grades and items you’ve seen, and typing backups stay in Safari’s extension storage. Requests go to your own Blackboard site with your existing session, plus a web font if you choose one and your background image if you set one.</p>
    <aside class="guide-callout"><strong>Blackboard remains the record.</strong> Glaze never submits coursework or changes grades. Check due dates and scores against Blackboard before relying on them.</aside>
    <p>Details are in the <a href={`${source}/blob/main/PRIVACY.md`}>privacy policy</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-section" id="troubleshooting">
    <h2>Recover, update or remove</h2>
    <ul class="guide-checklist">
      <li><strong>Glaze missing from Extensions:</strong> open Glaze.app from Applications once, then restart Safari.</li>
      <li><strong>Enabled but no theme:</strong> check Edit Websites for your Blackboard address, your Safari profile and private-browsing setting, then reload.</li>
      <li><strong>A page is hard to use:</strong> switch Glaze off to compare, go back to a built-in palette, and turn off the last Extra you changed.</li>
      <li><strong>No courses in the editor:</strong> open any Blackboard page with Glaze on, then reopen the editor.</li>
      <li><strong>“Not saved”:</strong> reopen the editor and check the value. Export before reinstalling.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Update</h3>
    <p>Download the newest disk image from <a href={`${source}/releases`}>GitHub Releases</a>, quit Safari, and replace Glaze.app in Applications. Open it once, then check that Glaze is still enabled and allowed.</p>
    <h3>Remove</h3>
    <p>Untick Glaze in Safari → Settings → Extensions to pause it. To remove it, export your settings, then delete Glaze.app from Applications.</p>
    <div class="guide-sources">
      <p>See also the <a href="/glaze">Glaze overview</a>, <a href="/guides/glaze-chrome">Chrome guide</a>, <a href={release}>release notes</a>, <a href={source}>source</a> and <a href={`${source}/issues`}>issue tracker</a>.</p>
      <p>Glaze is independent and not affiliated with or endorsed by Anthology Inc. or your institution.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
